{"name":"Science Experiment","hex":"#97a53f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"science experiment","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of extreme uncleanliness, especially one which involves food mess left for an excessive period of time that is liable to spoil, rot, mold, etc.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see science,‎ experiment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/science_experiment"}],"entries":[{"title":"Experiment","blocks":[{"text":"An experiment is a procedure carried out to support or refute a hypothesis, or determine the efficacy or likelihood of something previously untried. Experiments provide insight into cause-and-effect by demonstrating what outcome occurs when a particular factor is manipulated. Experiments vary greatly in goal and scale but always rely on repeatable procedure and logical analysis of the results. There also exist natural experimental studies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Apollo_15_feather_and_hammer_drop.ogv/330px--Apollo_15_feather_and_hammer_drop.ogv.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experiment","wikidata":"Q101965"},{"title":"Science","blocks":[{"text":"Science is a systematic discipline that builds and organises knowledge in the form of testable explanations about nature and society. It is driven by the scientific method: an empirical cycle that typically involves making observations, producing hypotheses, testing them with evidence, and drawing conclusions. Science encompasses this process and the body of knowledge it produces, which the scientific community continuously challenges, validates, and organises."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science","wikidata":"Q336"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Mirror_baby.jpg/500px-Mirror_baby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia